WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:01.329
Oscar Wilde

00:00:01.329 --> 00:00:02.329
His wit was legendary.

00:00:02.329 --> 00:00:06.270
His literary works showed signs of brilliance
and his lifestyle made him, for a time, among

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.730
the most celebrated artists of the age...

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:14.380
Yet his spectacular fall was unprecedented,
only equaled in modern times by the disaster

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.430
that was O.J. Simpson.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:21.910
In this week’s Biographics, we delve into
the outrageous life of (self proclaimed) genius

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:29.900
Oscar Wilde.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:37.020
Beginnings

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.780
Oscar Fingal O’Flahertie Wills Wilde entered
the world on October 16th, 1854.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.470
The flamboyance of his name was a portent
of things to come.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:51.110
His parents were socially prominent Anglo-Irish
Protestants, each with eclectic interests,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:56.380
a belief in national politics, and publishing
careers of their own.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.380
Oscar’s father, William, was a renowned
physician specializing in the eye and ear.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:04.570
He was a slight, unkempt figure with an ugly
beard and a roving eye.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:10.080
But that did not stop him carrying on numerous
affairs throughout his marriage, and he fathered

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.490
several illegitimate children.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:18.170
In contrast to her husband, Oscar’s mother,
Jane, was especially elegant and statuesque.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:21.230
Almost six feet tall, she towered over her
husband.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:26.490
She was a woman who ‘longed to make a sensation,’
once stating that ‘I should like to rage

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.010
through life - this orthodox creeping is too
tame for me.’

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:34.730
And, well, you probably guessed that this
was an attitude that her son would quickly

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:35.730
embrace…

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:40.130
When Oscar, her second son, was eight months
old, Jane described him as ‘a great stout

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.159
creature who minds nothing but growing fat.’

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:47.950
Jane had wanted a girl and is said to have
dressed and treated Oscar as a daughter for

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.700
the first decade of his life.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.729
At age nine, Oscar, along with his older brother
Willie, was sent to his first school - the

00:01:53.729 --> 00:01:58.680
Portora Royal Boarding School, in Enniskillen,
far in the Protestant north of Ireland.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.030
Oscar was younger than most of his peers.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:05.390
At first, he was eclipsed by his older brother,
but by the time that Willie was set to leave

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.810
Portora, he had been superseded academically
by Oscar.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:12.590
In fact, the younger Wilde was intellectually
far ahead of his classmates.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:13.960
In 1889 he recalled . . .

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.100
“I was looked upon as a prodigy by my associates
because, quite frequently, I would, for a

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:24.150
wager, read a three-volume novel in half an
hour so closely as to be able to give an accurate

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.600
resume of the plot of the story; by one hour’s
reading I was enabled to give a fair narrative

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.550
of the incidental scenes and the most pertinent
dialogue.”

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.870
Higher Education

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:45.730
In 1871, Wilde won a scholarship to study
Classics at Trinity College, Dublin.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.459
He arrived there in 1873, aged 18.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:53.660
There he was tutored and befriended by Reverend
John Pentland Mahaffy, Professor of Ancient

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:54.660
History.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:59.319
Mahaffy inspired his pupil to be proficient
in Greek and, in 1875, he won the prestigious

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:01.420
Berkeley Gold Medal in Greek.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.510
Three years later, in 1874, Oscar sailed to
England to take the examination for a Classics

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.770
scholarship at Oxford University.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.910
While awaiting the results, he went to London
and was dazzled by his first taste of the

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:13.910
metropolis.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:16.130
After that he headed off to another metropolis…

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.130
Paris.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.520
It was there, with his mother and brother,
that he received news that he had won his

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.520
scholarship.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.660
He’d not only won it, but he’d achieved
the highest mark of the entire group…

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.350
Oscar made the most of his time at Oxford.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:32.319
It was during this time that he cultivated
his aesthetic sensibilities, filling his room

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:37.121
with lilies and spending vast portions of
his father’s money upgrading the decor in

00:03:37.121 --> 00:03:38.121
his room.

00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:42.400
It was at this time that he famously said,
‘I find it harder and harder every day to

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.690
live up to my blue china.’

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:49.959
Wilde was never a fan of sport, but he enjoyed
watching others play cricket and run.

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:55.620
He developed a reputation for his wit, proclaiming
that ‘the only possible exercise is to talk,

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.620
not to walk.’

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.590
Still he was no pushover.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.620
It is believed that once, while in his room,
four undergraduates pounced on him to beat

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.740
him up and smash his belongings.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.340
Wilde is said to have kicked out the first
interloper, punched the second as he doubled

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.970
over, hurled the third through the air, and
carried the last back to his own room and

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:14.550
tossing him on the floor.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.430
It was during his time at Oxford that Wilde
became a Mason…

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:22.949
He adopted the costume - velvet breeches,
tailcoat, white tie and silk hose.

00:04:22.949 --> 00:04:29.349
But his extravagant lifestyle was dented in
April 1876 when his father died, leaving a

00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:31.139
serious debt behind.

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:34.939
Oscar though, he still managed to find a way
to indulge himself with semester holidays

00:04:34.939 --> 00:04:35.939
around Europe...

00:04:35.939 --> 00:04:41.669
Just prior to graduation in 1878, he consoled
his mother, distraught with financial worry

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:46.240
that ‘we have genius - that is something
attorney’s can’t take away.’

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.919
Oscar emerged with a degree from Oxford, and
a clear vision of what was in store.

00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:55.699
In a remarkably prophetic couple of sentences
he declared, ‘God knows, I won’t be a

00:04:55.699 --> 00:04:57.789
dried up Oxford don, anyhow.

00:04:57.789 --> 00:05:03.259
I cannot live without desire, fear and pain
. . . self-poised, self centered and self

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:04.259
comforted.

00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:06.069
I’ll be a poet, a writer, a dramatist.

00:05:06.069 --> 00:05:11.300
Somehow, or other, I’ll be famous, and if
not famous, notorious.’

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.520
In December 1878, Wilde moved to London.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.270
He shared a flat with Frank Miles, a fellow
Oxford graduate.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.979
During this time, he was introduced to many
writers, artists and actors.

00:05:21.979 --> 00:05:25.949
He soon gained a reputation for his wit, and
became a favored dinner guest, where he would

00:05:25.949 --> 00:05:28.229
espouse his aesthetic values.

00:05:28.229 --> 00:05:32.569
He applied for various fellowships and even
tried to become an inspector of schools.

00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:37.319
Meanwhile he was working on his first play,
Vera, which was privately printed in 1880.

00:05:37.319 --> 00:05:41.800
Vera was a story of noble socialism set in
19th Century Russia.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:50.289
But, unfortunately for Wilde, the London And
New York producers who Wilde sent the play

00:05:50.289 --> 00:05:54.159
to turned it down.

00:05:54.159 --> 00:05:56.089
Literary Beginnings

00:05:56.089 --> 00:06:01.689
In 1881, Oscar published sixty-one pieces
of writing under the title Poems.

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:07.310
An initial run of 750 copies sold out, with
two further printings being required.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.199
Meanwhile, a play by the renowned team of
Gilbert and Sullivan was, indirectly making

00:06:12.199 --> 00:06:13.229
Wilde famous.

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:17.599
It was called Patience and was a lampoon of
the aesthetic culture that Wilde epitomized.

00:06:17.599 --> 00:06:20.860
The main character was clearly modeled on
him.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:25.149
By June of 1881, Wilde’s status was such
that the Prince of Wales commented, ‘I do

00:06:25.149 --> 00:06:29.599
not know Mr. Wilde, and not to know Mr. Wilde
is not to be known.’

00:06:29.599 --> 00:06:34.650
Despite his ever growing reputation, Wilde
found himself in tough financial straits.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:39.289
He was offered, and accepted, a series of
lecture tours around America to coincide with

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:41.440
the New York opening of Patience.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:47.849
He set sail on Christmas Eve 1881 to instruct
the New World in ‘The English Art of Renaissance.’

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:54.009
On arrival, he told a New York customs officer,
‘I have nothing to declare but my genius.’

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:55.030
Don’t try this today anyone…

00:06:55.030 --> 00:07:00.369
Well, the Americans were fascinated by him
and the original schedule would be extended

00:07:00.369 --> 00:07:03.410
repeatedly in response to public demand.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:07.159
It would last almost a year, and even extended
to Canada.

00:07:07.159 --> 00:07:09.930
Financially, he did very well out of the lecture
tour.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.889
He stayed in New York for two months after
the tour’s end.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:17.960
He then briefly returned to London in January
1883, before relocating to Paris, where he

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.229
immersed himself in artistic circles.

00:07:20.229 --> 00:07:22.699
He now made himself over physically.

00:07:22.699 --> 00:07:27.589
He had his flowing locks transformed into
a bowl haircut and took to wearing a black

00:07:27.589 --> 00:07:28.679
overcoat...

00:07:28.679 --> 00:07:33.740
This was in great contrast to the flamboyant
colors that he was known for.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:38.740
In May, 1883, Wilde returned to London, apparently
motivated by his interest in a woman named

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.969
Constance Lloyd, the daughter of a prosperous
London lawyer.

00:07:41.969 --> 00:07:47.779
For the last few years, despite his American
earnings, Oscar had had money worries.

00:07:47.779 --> 00:07:53.809
The right marriage might solve them, while
also answering growing gossip about his sexual

00:07:53.809 --> 00:07:54.809
character.

00:07:54.809 --> 00:08:00.069
A year-long courtship followed, with the marriage
taking place on 29th May, 1884.

00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:04.189
They honeymooned in Paris and then, thanks
to his new father-in-law’s money, occupied

00:08:04.189 --> 00:08:06.300
a four-story house in London.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.559
He then went ahead and had the place redecorated
at huge expense, which plunged the newlyweds

00:08:11.559 --> 00:08:12.970
into immediate debt.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:19.119
Wilde’s first child, Cyril, was born on
June 5th, 1885 with Vyvyan following on November

00:08:19.119 --> 00:08:21.080
5th, 1886.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:25.559
With his writing career going nowhere, he
agreed to a British lecture tour with topics

00:08:25.559 --> 00:08:28.469
such as ‘The Value of Art in Modern Life.’

00:08:28.469 --> 00:08:33.440
Lecturing and other invitations kept him away
from the family home.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:38.039
During his long absences he began to surround
himself with young men, writing unguardedly

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:41.230
of his infatuation with the beauty of the
male form.

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:48.780
He formed an especially close friendship with
a seventeen-year-old named Robbie Ross…

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:55.550
Success &amp; Scandal

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:59.120
During the late 1880’s, while bringing in
money with occasional book reviews, Wilde

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.530
worked on his first novel, The Picture of
Dorian Gray.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:06.810
When the book was published in a magazine
in 1890 it caused an immediate scandal.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:11.680
The storyline involved a subtly eroticized
triangle of relationships between three men

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.530
and was condemned by many as being immoral.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:18.180
Wilde had predicted such an outcome, having
written the following in the preface “There

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.770
is no such thing as a moral or an immoral
book.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.930
Books are well written or badly written.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:23.930
That is all.”

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:28.200
It was around this time that Wilde was introduced
to a blond fair skinned undergraduate by the

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.710
name of Lord Alfred Douglas, but who was known
to his intimates as Bosie.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:35.430
Oscar quickly became obsessed with the twenty-two-year
old…

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:38.800
From this time on, Oscar saw Constance and
his children less often.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:43.600
He did his writings from rented addresses
or hotels, usually with Bosie in tow....

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.440
Bosie’s father was the Marquis of Queensbury,
the man who developed the rules of professional

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:49.440
boxing.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.740
He took an immediate and intense exception
to the relationship between his son and the

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:54.940
famous Wilde.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:59.040
Apart from the homosexual aspect of their
relationship, Queensbury was enraged that

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.130
Wilde was distracting his son from his studies
at Oxford.

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:08.420
When Bosie quit his studies in May, 1893,
the Marquis became determined to bring down

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:09.420
Wilde.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.370
It was around this time that Wilde finally
achieved success on the stage.

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:17.880
A Woman of No Importance opened on April 19th,
1893 to widespread acclaim.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:22.540
During its long run, it brought him between
170 and 200 pounds per week.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:27.370
His next play, the satire An Ideal Husband,
was also successful, providing the means for

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:31.260
Oscar and Bosie to travel widely and live
extravagantly.

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:35.790
The Marquis of Queensbury thought his son’s
failure to take his degree in Oxford was a

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:36.860
scandalous waste of time.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:42.300
He placed the blame squarely at the feet of
Wilde, referring to the relationship as the

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:45.500
‘most loathsome and disgusting’.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.390
Queensbury began hounding the pair incessantly.

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:50.470
He threatened to disinherit Boise.

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:54.740
To this the son replied in a telegram, ‘What
a funny little man you are!’

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:59.730
Queensbury didn’t like this one bit, and
on the 19th of June, Queensbury burst in on

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.070
Wilde at his Tithe Street address in London.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:06.700
He had a bodyguard with him and proceeded
to threaten bodily harm unless the relationship

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:07.860
was immediately ended.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.810
And what was Bosie reaction to all this?

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:13.720
Well, he wrote nonchalantly to his father,
‘I write to inform you that I treat your

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.870
absurd threats with absolute indifference.’

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:21.100
The encounter though, it unsettled Wilde,
who got out of London for several months,

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.420
going on holiday with his family to Worthing.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:27.340
This is where he worked on his latest play,
The Importance of Being Earnest.

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:32.470
But it wasn’t long before Bosie, unable
to stay away, invited himself along…

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:39.300
As you might imagine this caused a certain
degree of tension between Mr. and Mrs. Wilde.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.010
Trials

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:46.280
The Importance of Being Earnest opened to
great applause on February 14th, 1895.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.340
Two weeks later it was already a ‘wild’
success…

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:54.640
In true Oscar WIlde style is began basking
in the glow of this, Wilde called in at the

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.410
lavish Albemarle Club...

00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:59.430
There he was handed a card from Queensbury
by a porter.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:03.310
On this card was scrawled, ‘To Oscar Wilde,
posing sodomite.’

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:07.390
At Bosie’s urging, Wilde went to Marlborough
Street police station to ask for a warrant

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:09.540
for Queensbury’s arrest on grounds of libel.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.100
The case came to trial on April 3rd.

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:15.690
Prosecuting Counsel, Edward Carson put on
the stand a sixteen-year-old ‘rent boy’

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:18.430
who claimed to have been paid for sex by Wilde.

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:23.400
Carson then went on to dissect Wilde’s published
works, revealing their supposed homosexual

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:24.400
undertones...

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:27.320
And how did WIlde react to this serious event?

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:31.380
Well, he chose to wield his celebrated wit
as his main defensive tool...

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.750
He was often funny, but the implicit superiority
in his position was also damaging.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:39.500
In one exchange, when Carson asked whether
the affection and love that is portrayed in

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:44.300
The Picture of Dorian Gray might lead an ordinary
individual to believe it had a sodomitical

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:48.800
tendency, Oscar replied, ‘I have no knowledge
of the ordinary individual.’

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.600
Not exactly endearing himself to anyone…

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:57.340
Wilde’s counsel, Edward Clarke, made serious
miscalculations that did his client no favors.

00:12:57.340 --> 00:13:00.880
He went over letters that Queensberry had
sent to his son in an attempt to show how

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.320
crazed the father had become...

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:07.060
To the jury, though, Queensbury’s volatility
was driven by a paternal regard for Bosie's

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:08.060
character.

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:12.730
As Carson put it, his client had one hope
alone, and that was simply saving his son.

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:16.250
On the third day of proceedings, with things
going not exactly brilliantly...

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:18.260
Wilde chose not to attend.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:22.590
This was to prove the most damaging day yet,
with Carson announcing that he intended to

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:27.940
introduce a number of boys who would testify
to ‘shocking acts’ performed by Oscar...

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:31.410
Without consulting with his client, Wild’s
lawyer, Clarke, offered to abandon the case.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.770
The defense, however, insisted that the original
plea stand.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:38.080
The judge agreed and compelled an acquittal
from the jury.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.630
Queensbury was found not guilty of libel against
Wilde.

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:44.320
It had now been proven that his written accusation
of sodomy was not libelous.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:47.670
Now this had another important consequence…

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:53.250
It Wilde highly vulnerable to arrest for sodomy,
which was a crime in England at the time.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:54.610
And, well this happened…

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:59.510
At five in the evening of the day that the
libel case was decided, a summons was issued

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.020
for the arrest of Oscar Wilde.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:04.340
The charge was ‘committing acts of gross
indecency.’

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:08.970
Hiding in the Cadogan Hotel, Oscar was urged
by Bosie and others to take a boat immediately

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:10.020
for France…

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:11.850
Even his wife told him to run.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.420
However this just didn’t sit right with
Wilde.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:19.020
He was determined to stand his ground, declaring,
‘I shall stay and do my sentence, whatever

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:20.020
it is.’

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:24.920
At 6:10 pm, two detectives arrived, and took
a semi-drunk Wilde to Bow Street station.

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:27.980
The arrest of Oscar Wilde caused an absolute
sensation.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.460
Any friends that he still had quickly drifted
away.

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:35.170
Both of his currently running plays were cancelled
and his name very quickly became toxic.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:38.770
Wilde was kept in a cell in Bow Street (then
in Holloway Prison).

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:42.990
Queensbury now administered the low blow of
forcing a bankruptcy sale of Oscar’s belongings,

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:45.150
helped by a long list of angry creditors.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:48.100
By this time, Wilde was about £6000 in debt.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:52.410
After making sure that Oscar had nothing of
any physical value left, Queensbury wrote

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:56.020
to a newspaper denying that he was capable
of any sympathy for Wilde.

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:59.020
He stated ‘I have helped to cut up and destroy
sharks.

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:02.410
I have no sympathy for them, but may have
felt sorry, and wished to put them out of

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:04.150
pain as fast as possible.’

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.430
The trial ran from the 26th to the 29th of
April.

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:12.170
It was extraordinarily explicit in its allusions
to sexual acts, many coming from the young

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:14.700
men who claimed to have been partakers.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:18.570
Various Savoy Hotel employees testified that
they had seen boys in Wilde’s bed.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:22.980
However, Oscar’s counsel was able to point
out contradictions in the testimonies, especially

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:24.790
those of the ‘rent boys.’

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:27.790
This all meant that the jury were unable to
reach a verdict.

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:32.589
But WIlde was not out of the woods, as an
almost immediate retrial began.

00:15:32.589 --> 00:15:37.120
Between the two trials, he managed to secure
bail, though stiff conditions were imposed...

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:40.900
He tried to stay at a number of hotels, only
to be told at each that he was unwelcome.

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.700
He finally managed to find lodgings with his
brother Willie.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.820
The second trial began on May 22nd at the
Old Bailey.

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:52.680
This time, after two hours of discussion,
the jury returned with a verdict of guilty.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:53.740
The sentence?

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:58.240
Two years hard labor.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:01.040
Confinement

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:02.700
Wilde was taken to Pentonville Prison.

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.870
The wooden bed in his cell had sheets and
rugs but no mattress.

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:08.270
A tin pot was provided for his toilet.

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:13.710
The prison clothes were not exactly his accustomed
style of dress...

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:19.300
Wilde was compelled to walk a treadmill senselessly
for six hours each day, and allowed to exercise

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:20.900
outdoors for one hour.

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:22.990
He was also forced to make postal bags.

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:27.670
For three whole months he had no outside contact,
and after that things were not much better,

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:30.040
with him only being allowed to write one letter.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:36.170
The regime was brutal...Wilde recalling three
punishments authorized by law - hunger, insomnia

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:37.170
and illness.

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:42.350
In September 1895, he received a visit from
Constance who found the experience ‘awful,

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:44.830
more so than any conception of it could be.’

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:49.440
Constance reported that Wilde professed a
rejection of his former conduct, and begged

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:52.710
her forgiveness for his madness during the
last three years.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:56.370
She decided to stand by her ‘weak rather
than wicked’ spouse.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:17:00.630
Still, she abandoned his name, referring to
herself as Constance Holland.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:05.170
When Bosie heard about Oscar’s rejection
of his former lifestyle he was heartbroken.

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:09.879
He wrote, ‘I am not in prison but I think
I suffer as much as Oscar and in fact more.’

00:17:09.879 --> 00:17:11.459
That was probably not the case though…

00:17:11.459 --> 00:17:14.209
I mean, prison was pretty rough.

00:17:14.209 --> 00:17:16.360
In October, he actually came down with dysentery.

00:17:16.360 --> 00:17:19.970
On 21st November, he was transferred to Reading
Prison.

00:17:19.970 --> 00:17:24.020
At Clapham Junction station, he was spat on
and ridiculed by the crowds.

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:27.629
Here though conditions were improved and his
duties were lighter.

00:17:27.629 --> 00:17:31.259
He was released on May 19th, 1896.

00:17:31.259 --> 00:17:35.409
Oscar was booked into the Hotel Sandwich in
Dieppe, Northern France, living off the generosity

00:17:35.409 --> 00:17:37.269
of the few friends who had stood by him.

00:17:37.269 --> 00:17:42.249
By now, Constance had decided on divorce and,
suffering herself with spinal paralysis, put

00:17:42.249 --> 00:17:43.700
off any reunion.

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:49.429
Between June and July 1897 Oscar wrote his
last major work, The Ballad of Reading Goal.

00:17:49.429 --> 00:17:50.429
The subject?

00:17:50.429 --> 00:17:54.659
Well that would be underlining the need for
reform in Britain’s prison and justice systems…

00:17:54.659 --> 00:17:57.299
Life in Dieppe was excruciatingly lonely for
Oscar.

00:17:57.299 --> 00:18:01.379
Society at large shunned him and he spent
day after day alone and miserable.

00:18:01.379 --> 00:18:05.659
Finally, his resolve collapsed and he wrote
to Bosie, inviting him to come and stay.

00:18:05.659 --> 00:18:10.530
They reconciled on August 28th, with Wilde
bursting into tears at the sight of his ‘one

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:11.600
true love.’

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:15.929
When Constance heard of her husband’s behaviour
she wrote to him forbidding any return ‘to

00:18:15.929 --> 00:18:18.080
your filthy, insane life.’

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:21.070
When he refused to give up Bosie, she cut
him off completely...

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:25.429
Once the initial passion subsided, it became
clear that both Wilde and Bosie were very

00:18:25.429 --> 00:18:27.620
different than their pre-trial selves.

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:31.950
Bosie felt a duty to help Wide, but this was
no longer tied to love.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.669
On December 3rd, Bosie left for Naples, and
did not return.

00:18:35.669 --> 00:18:39.100
Oscar’s last two years - mostly in Paris
- where pitiful.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:41.600
He drank, hired men, and begged.

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:42.900
Acquaintances were few.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:48.129
Constance Holland sadly died age forty after
an operation on her back on April, 7th, 1898.

00:18:48.129 --> 00:18:53.340
Then, in April, 1900 Wilde went with an acquaintance
named Harold Mellor to Rome, where he received

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:54.340
the Pope’s blessing.

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:58.700
A month later he was in Paris and suffering
from ill-health, which he attributed to food

00:18:58.700 --> 00:18:59.700
poisoning.

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:01.970
His skin was irritable, frequently coming
out in rashes.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:04.379
By September, he was completely bedridden.

00:19:04.379 --> 00:19:07.450
On October 10th, he was operated upon for
an ear infection.

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:10.200
The operation did not go well and he got worse.

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:13.899
Still he insisted on drinking absinthe and
champagne.

00:19:13.899 --> 00:19:19.090
On November 25th a diagnosis of acute, inoperable
cerebral meningitis was reached.

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:22.519
From here on, Oscar drifted in and out of
consciousness and even sanity.

00:19:22.519 --> 00:19:26.529
At half past five the following morning, a
death rattle began.

00:19:26.529 --> 00:19:31.409
Minutes after his demise, his body actually
sort-of exploded, fluid pouring out of several

00:19:31.409 --> 00:19:32.590
orifices.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:38.580
Oscar Wilde died as he had lived - inappropriately,
outrageously and with scant reference to what

00:19:38.580 --> 00:20:22.839
decorum demanded.

